window.docContents[198].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems":{"contents":"Energy arguments produce solutions without explicit formulas. Green's first identity\non a coordinate box already shows the mechanism: for the difference  of two\n solutions with the same boundary values, the identity collapses to\n, and uniqueness follows without any representation\nformula. The weak theory keeps that argument and drops the classical\nregularity. On  the Dirichlet form\n is bounded, the Poincaré inequality of the\nSobolev chapter makes it coercive on bounded domains, and Lax–Milgram returns a\nunique weak solution of  for every , together\nwith an a priori estimate, continuous dependence on the load, and a\ncharacterization as the minimizer of the Dirichlet energy. For classical\ncompetitors on a bounded domain with  graph boundary, the\nclassical Dirichlet principle\ngives the same characterization.\n\nThe same pattern extends to variable principal coefficients, where\nalmost-everywhere ellipticity yields the integrated coercivity, and to\nlower-order terms, where coercivity can fail and a Gårding inequality with a\nspectral shift takes its place. The modified Helmholtz problem\n with  works on any measurable domain:\nthe gradient and mass terms together control the full  norm without a\nPoincaré inequality. The shifted\nlower-order solver is the input of the Fredholm section, and the weak solutions\nbuilt here are what the regularity section improves.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations","header":"5.4. Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Canonical-Euclidean-spacetime":{"contents":"We fix one spacetime type rather than accept whichever presentation of\n a lemma arrives in. EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) is\nWithLp 2 (Fin n → ℝ), and the WithLp structure keeps it distinct from the\nfunction type Fin n → ℝ with its maximum norm; PiLp.continuousLinearEquiv\nconverts between them. The Zulip discussion\nthoughts on gradient\ngives background on these normed-space instance choices. We pin the\nEuclidean type and convert at the boundary, so the projections land in\nEuclidean coordinates rather than in a function space.\n\nThe coordinate functions are bundled as continuous linear mapswith exact coordinate lawsThe bundled spatial projection has the same coordinate values as the plain\nprojection.The mapis the derivative of every fixed-time embedding\n. Its coordinate laws are\n and .Construction.\nThe time map is Mathlib's continuous-linear coordinate projection at\n. For the spatial map, take the family of\ncoordinate projections indexed by , using\n in spacetime, assemble the family into a continuous linear map to a\nfunction space, and transport it through the continuous-linear equivalence\nbetween that function space and Euclidean  coordinates. Extensional\nequality with spatialPart follows by evaluating both sides at every .\nThe embedding is verified in coordinate zero and in every successor\ncoordinate.\n\nThe bundled projections combine into an explicit equivalence between canonical\nspacetime and the product . Both directions are\ngiven by formulas, so round-trip identities are available for rewriting.\n\nCanonical spacetime has two explicit product views:They are inverse:Both directions are continuous linear maps, and together they defineComposing with the product swap gives the equally useful equivalenceThe names encode the order: spaceTimeContinuousLinearEquiv returns\n, while timeSpaceContinuousLinearEquiv returns , so one\npair is never read in both orders.Verification.\nFor the first round trip, compare the spatial and time components:\nspatialPart (timeSpaceCons t x) = x and the zeroth coordinate of\ntimeSpaceCons t x is . For the reverse round trip, use coordinate\nextensionality. Coordinate zero is reconstructed from ; every successor\ncoordinate is reconstructed from the corresponding entry of spatialPart z.The bundled equivalence is a composite of four Mathlib equivalences: Euclidean\ncoordinates to plain functions, splitting coordinate zero off the tail,\nreturning the tail to Euclidean coordinates, and swapping the two factors.\nIts forward and inverse maps unfold to the split and join maps by\ndefinition, and the time-first equivalence adds one more swap.\n\nAt the level of functions on spacetime, the same coordinate round trip converts\nbetween curried fields  and fields on .\n\nFor any codomain type , with no topology or algebraic structure required,\nthe coordinate round trip inducesExplicitly,Both composites are identity functions. Since this is a coordinate\nreindexing, scalar fields, vector fields, predicates, solution records, and\nsymbolic objects can all use the same interface.Verification.\nFor a curried , evaluate at arbitrary  and use the two projection\nlaws for . For a spacetime field , evaluate at arbitrary  and\nuse the reconstruction identity\n. Function extensionality then gives both global\nequalities.\n\nBecause the coordinate equivalence is continuous linear, it is , and\ncomposing with it preserves every order of differentiability. Smoothness can\ntherefore be checked in whichever of the two coordinate presentations is\nconvenient.\n\nLet  be a real normed vector space and let .\nSmoothness is invariant under the spacetime coordinate equivalence:Equivalently, a curried field  is jointly  after\nspaceTimeUncurry exactly when the ordinary product function\n is . This is joint regularity; it is stronger\nthan assuming separate smoothness in  and .\n\nBoth directions are composition with one side of the continuous-linear\nequivalence\n. Continuous linear maps\nare , so composition preserves every requested order .\nThe inverse laws identify the resulting composites with the original fields.\nThe curried statement is the same theorem after unfolding spaceTimeUncurry.\n\nThe first differential operator adapted to the spacetime convention is the\nspatial gradient, which drops the time component of the full gradient. It is the\noperator appearing in the heat and transport equations, where the time\nderivative is treated separately.\n\nFor a scalar spacetime field , the spatial\ngradient discards the time component of the full Euclidean gradient:Its exact component formula isCoordinate zero never appears. As with the full gradient and\npartialDeriv, this is an equality of totalized expressions; interpreting\nthe components as classical derivatives requires differentiability of \nat the point.Verification.\nEvaluate the spatial projection at , which selects coordinate  of\nthe full gradient. The gradient-coordinate theorem attached to the gradient\ndefinition identifies that coordinate with .\n\nEvolution equations differentiate a field in time at fixed  and in space at\nfixed . The slice derivative theorems express both as the joint Fréchet\nderivative evaluated on a coordinate direction, under differentiability of the\njoint field at the point.\n\nLet  be a real normed vector space. At a point where the joint field is\ndifferentiable, derivatives of fixed-space and fixed-time slices are the\ncorresponding joint directional derivatives.For a product field ,andFor a canonical spacetime field ,The same facts are also stated after pulling  back through\njoinSpaceTime: the product direction  becomes spacetime coordinate\n, and  becomes coordinate .\n\nApply the Fréchet chain rule to the affine inclusions\n and . Their derivatives are respectively\n and . Evaluating at  or  gives\nthe two product formulas.For canonical spacetime, the fixed-time embedding has derivative\n, and\n. The fixed-space time curve has velocity .\nSubstitution into the same chain rule yields the coordinate formulas.\nFinally, joinSpaceTime is the inverse continuous linear map from\n coordinates, so the pullback statements are these identities in the\nproduct representation.\n\nOnce slice derivatives are written as fderiv on a constant direction, the\nregularity of a slice derivative follows from that of the derivative field,\nwhich has one order less than the field. Each statement fixes which variable is\nallowed to move, and none of them upgrades a fixed-time result to a joint one.\n\nThe slice operations use exactly the expected number of derivatives.\nIf  is globally , then:* for every fixed , the map\n   is ;* the jointly varying spatial derivative\n   is ;* consequently, for every fixed , the time traceis .If  is , then a fixed-point time trace of two spatial\nderivatives is . These are the exact variable dependencies of the Lean\ntheorems.There is also a local cylinder statement. If  is open\nand  is jointly  on , then every curve\n is differentiable at every . Thus a classical\nsolution predicate that already stores joint  regularity should not\nstore time differentiability again as independent data.\n\nRewrite each slice derivative using the preceding slice chain rules as\nfderiv of  evaluated on a constant direction. The derivative field of\na  map is ; evaluation of a continuous-linear map on a\nconstant vector preserves that regularity. Composing with the smooth paths\n or  gives the trace statements. Iterate the\nargument for two spatial derivatives. On an open cylinder, joint ContDiffOn\nupgrades to ContDiffAt at each interior point, after which the fixed-space\nchain rule supplies differentiability of the time curve.\n\nCommuting a time derivative with spatial derivatives is the slice form of the\nsymmetry of mixed partials, and needs the corresponding global  or \nregularity.\n\nFor a globally  field\n, one spatial derivative commutes with\nthe derivative of a fixed-space time trace:For a globally  field, the same is true after two spatial derivatives:The codomain  is any real normed space. After slice derivatives are rewritten as\njoint derivatives, the  and  hypotheses provide the symmetric\nsecond differentials of the field and of its spatial derivatives that move time past one or\ntwo spatial directions.\n\nRepresent the left side using the constant spatial direction  and\nthe time direction . Both directions are constant, so differentiating\nagain gives the second differential on the pair, and its symmetry exchanges\ntheir order, proving the  identity. For two spatial derivatives, first\nview the inner spatial derivative as a new  field; one derivative has\nalready been spent from the original  hypothesis. Apply the first\ncommutation theorem and then commute time past the remaining spatial\ndirection.\n\nThe last spacetime statement is topological rather than differential: a\ncontinuous field is bounded on every compact cylinder\n. Bounds of this kind feed the integrability and\ndominated-convergence arguments of the evolution chapters.\n\nLet  be continuous, where  is a normed additive\ngroup. For every closed time interval  and closed spatial ball\n, there is a nonnegative  such thatNo assumption that  or  is needed. If either set is empty,\nthe conclusion is vacuous; otherwise it is the ordinary compact-cylinder\nbound.\n\nCompose  with the jointly smooth assembly map\n. The product of a closed interval and a closed ball in\nfinite-dimensional Euclidean space is compact. A continuous norm-valued\nfunction is bounded on that compact set. Replacing the resulting bound by\nits maximum with zero gives the stated nonnegative constant.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Euclidean, polar, and spherical coordinates","header":"3.3.1.1. Canonical Euclidean spacetime","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Canonical-Euclidean-spacetime"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-driven-linear-instability":{"contents":"Diffusion damps each scalar Fourier mode of the heat equation. In a coupled\nsystem, unequal diffusion rates can instead destabilize a reaction matrix\nwhose spatially homogeneous evolution is stable. For\n\n\n\na cosine of frequency  evolves by the matrix .\nThe theorem finds a growing mode from the raw coefficient inequalities.\n\nSuppose  and, writing ,Then the homogeneous ODE is asymptotically stable, but the linear PDE on\nthe line admits arbitrarily small uniformly bounded initial perturbations\nwhose first component at the origin grows without bound in magnitude. The\nconstructed solutions are cosine modes  with\n. There is also an explicitly chosen interval\n on which these solutions have zero Neumann derivatives at both\nendpoints and spatially nonconstant initial data.The interval length is determined by the unstable frequency, and the\ninstability is that of the linear system.\n\nThe trace and determinant of  give homogeneous asymptotic stability.\nFor a spatial mode with , the determinant becomes\n. The strict inequalities make this quadratic\nnegative for some , so the mode matrix has a positive real\neigenvalue  and a nonzero eigenvector with nonzero first\ncomponent. Multiplying that eigenvector by a sufficiently small scalar\ngives any requested initial uniform bound, while \nforces growth at the origin. Setting the interval length to \nmakes the sine factors in both boundary derivatives vanish.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations","header":"6.6. Diffusion-driven linear instability","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-driven-linear-instability"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Traces-into-fractional-spaces":{"contents":"The trace chapter constructs the flat trace\n and its\n analogue, and introduces their intrinsic trace spaces as kernel\nquotients. The fractional scales of this chapter identify those flat ranges.\nAt  the range is the Fourier space ; for\n it is the Gagliardo space of order .\n\nThe Fourier route starts on the whole space. For a Schwartz function  on\n, the Fourier transform of  is the\nintegral of  over the normal frequency .\nCauchy–Schwarz against the weight  gives\n\n\n\nfinite exactly when . This loses half a derivative, and no more.\nThe trace constant is , called traceConstant s in Lean.\n\nLet . There are bounded real-linear mapswith ,  and\n. In particular  is surjective. If  is\nrepresented by a Schwartz function , the physical representative of\n is  almost everywhere.\n\nIn weighted Fourier coordinates, integration against a unit  normal\nkernel is contractive; multiplying by  gives the physical trace.\nThe corresponding kernel lifting is isometric before multiplication by\n. Fubini and the unit normalization give the right-inverse\nidentity and the exact lifting norm. Agreement with pointwise restriction\nfor Schwartz functions uses the Fourier slice identity. Both maps preserve\nHermitian symmetry of the Fourier transform, so they restrict to the real\nsubspaces.\n\nOn the half-space, even reflection carries  into\n, where the integer-order comparison of this chapter\nidentifies it with Fourier . Applying  then gives a trace with\nvalues in .\n\nThere are bounded linear mapssuch that the physical  representative of \nis , and .\nSo every element of real  is the trace of a half-space\n function, and  has the same kernel as\n. On restrictions of whole-space functions,\n agrees with . Both norm bounds are explicit\nproducts of the constants of the maps involved.\n\nThe trace is the composite of even reflection, the integer-order\nequivalence and . Its physical value is \nbecause the reflected function restricts back to  and the whole-space\ntrace of a restriction is its half-space trace. The\nlifting is  followed by restriction to the half-space. Injectivity of\nthe physical representative on  turns the physical identity into\nthe kernel identity.\n\nThe range of  in  is exactly the\nset of physical representatives of real . The\nquotient  of the trace chapter is\nisomorphic to  by a continuous linear\nequivalence compatible with the two boundary realizations in , and the\nquotient norm and the Bessel norm bound each other with explicit constants.\n\nSince  and  have the same\nkernel,  descends to the quotient; the lifting\nfollowed by the quotient map is its inverse. The forward bound applies the\ntrace bound to the minimal representative, and the reverse bound uses\n as one representative of the class of .\n\nFor  there is no Fourier characterization to use, and the boundary\nspace is the Gagliardo space of order  with exponent . Its\ndefining energy for a trace  is\n\n\n\nFor smooth , split  through the two points at height\n above  and . The two vertical pieces are controlled by\nHardy's inequality in the normal variable and the horizontal piece by a\ntranslation estimate. Density and Fatou's lemma pass the bound to\n.\n\nLet  and ; the case  is trivial, since the energy\nvanishes on a one-point boundary. There is a constant  such that for\nevery  the energy above is at most\n. Consequently  satisfies\nthe Gagliardo condition of order , and the trace defines a bounded\nlinear mapwhose first coordinate is the physical  trace and whose norm is at\nmost , with the same  as in the energy bound. At  it\nagrees with the Hilbert trace .\n\nThe smooth estimate is the splitting described above. For general ,\napproximate by smooth compactly supported functions, pass to an almost\neverywhere convergent subsequence of traces, and apply Fatou's lemma to the\nenergy integral. The norm bound adds the  trace estimate to the\nenergy estimate.\n\nThe inverse direction is an explicit extension. Given boundary data ,\nset\n\n\n\nwhere  is a mollifier at scale  and  is a cutoff equal\nto one near  and vanishing for . The slices converge to \nin  as , and the derivatives of  are controlled by\nthe Gagliardo energy of .\n\nLet . For every mollifier ,  is a bounded linear\nmap from the Gagliardo space of order  into\n, and it is a right inverse of the trace of the\nprevious theorem. Hence that trace is surjective, and a function\n satisfies the Gagliardo condition of order\n if and only if it agrees almost everywhere with the trace of some\n.\n\nThe slices of  converge to  in , which identifies the\ntrace. The tangential derivatives are zero-mean kernel fields times the\ncutoff, and the Gagliardo energy of  bounds them. The normal derivative\nhas one more term, from the derivative of the cutoff, which the  norm\nof  bounds.\n\nFor , the finite-exponent trace quotient of the trace chapter\nis isomorphic, by a continuous linear equivalence, to\n. The equivalence\nsends the class of  to its fractional trace, its  coordinate is the\nphysical boundary realization, and its norm and the norm of its inverse are\nbounded by the norms of the trace and of the standard mollified lifting.\n\nThe fractional trace and the physical trace have the same kernel, so the\nfractional trace descends to the quotient. The standard lifting followed by\nthe quotient map is its inverse by the right-inverse identity and injectivity\nof the quotient's boundary realization.\n\nThe estimate, the lifting and the right-inverse identity use only the norm\nand the linear structure of the target, so they hold for boundary data in a\nBanach space.\n\nLet  be a real Banach space and . The trace of\n satisfies the Gagliardo condition of order\n with values in , and defines a bounded linear map into\n whose kernel is the\nkernel of the physical trace. For every mollifier the -valued mollified\nlifting is a bounded right inverse, so this trace is surjective.\n\nFor a boundary difference  with , pass through the\npoints at height  above  and above . The two vertical legs are\ncontrolled by Hardy's inequality in the normal variable, applied to the real\nfunction , and the horizontal leg at\nheight  by a tangential translation estimate. Integrating against\n gives the fractional energy bound for compactly supported\n functions, with a constant depending only on  and . Compact\nsmooth density, an almost everywhere convergent subsequence of boundary\nvalues, and Fatou's lemma for nonnegative integrands extend the bound to the\nSobolev trace; Fatou keeps the constant. The lifting is the cutoff\nmollification of the scalar case with a Bochner integral in place of the\nreal one. No approximation property of  is used.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.13. Traces into fractional spaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Traces-into-fractional-spaces"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Symbolic-Calculus-and-Analytic-Bridges":{"contents":"\n\nFor a scalar field , the coordinate derivative is\n: evaluate its Fréchet derivative on the\nth basis vector. A symbolic derivative must recover this same linear\napproximation. The partial-derivative bridge proves that it does, under the domain\nconditions of the expression being differentiated. For example,\n is valid whenever\n; neither coordinate has to be nonzero on its own.\n\nAt every point satisfying the generated validity conditions, symbolic\ndifferentiation in coordinate  agrees with our analytic Euclidean\npartial derivative:\n.\n\nStructural induction on the expression constructs its Fréchet derivative\nusing the analytic sum, product, quotient, power, and elementary chain rules.\nThe validity hypotheses discharge each rule's domain conditions. Evaluating\nthe resulting continuous linear map on  gives exactly the evaluated\nsymbolic derivative, hence the stated coordinate identity.\n\nThe validity predicate that justifies one symbolic derivative also certifies\nregularity of every order. At a point where ValidAt holds, evaluation of a\nreflected expression is  for every smoothness order , the analytic\norder included, because each constructor is interpreted by an elementary\nfunction that Mathlib proves smooth of every order away from its\nsingularities; the On and global forms assume validity at every point of\nthe set or the space. The theorems are stated for the two conventions of the\ntheorem-facing tactics, functions on  and functions on\n with frozen parameters, and they are what cas applies\nto a DifferentiableAt, Differentiable, ContDiffAt, ContDiffOn, or\nContDiff goal:\n\nStructural induction on the expression. Constants and coordinate projections\nare smooth; sums, products, and negations preserve ContDiffAt; quotients,\nlogarithms, square roots, real powers, and the inverse sine and cosine are\nsmooth at every point satisfying the validity clause of their constructor, by\nMathlib's ContDiffAt lemmas for the elementary functions and the field\noperations; the remaining elementary functions are smooth everywhere. The derivative bridge also gives differentiability, and the set and global\nforms quantify these pointwise statements over their domain.\n\nAt a point where the scalar inputs being differentiated are valid, the\ncoordinatewise bridge gives gradient, divergence, and three-dimensional curl\nformulas. The Laplacian bridge additionally assumes validity of every first\nsymbolic partial at that point. Hessians, Jacobians, symmetric\ngradients, tensor divergence, and vector Laplacians use the same finite-indexed\nconstruction. In particular,\n,\n,\n,\nand\n.\n\nFor each coordinate,  identifies\nevaluation of the reflected partial derivative with the corresponding\nanalytic derivative. The reflected gradient is the vector of these\ncoordinates, divergence is their finite sum, the Laplacian is the sum of the\ndiagonal second derivatives, and curl is the stated alternating combination.\nFunction extensionality and finite-sum congruence assemble the coordinate\nidentities into the four displayed operator identities.For the Laplacian, applying the first-derivative identity only at \nwould not suffice: two functions can agree at one point and have different\nderivatives there. Validity persists on a neighborhood of , so the\nanalytic first partial and the evaluated symbolic first partial agree\nthroughout that neighborhood. Their derivatives at  therefore agree.\nValidity of the first symbolic partial justifies the second application of\nthe derivative bridge. These are sufficient conditions on the intermediate\nformulas; the largest domain of smoothness can be bigger.\n\nIf the advected scalar expression is valid at the evaluation point,\nchecked symbolic advection evaluates directly to our analytic\nadvectiveDerivative. The componentwise convection and source-subtracted\ntransport residual theorems use the same bridge, so later proofs need not\ndepend on the reflected evaluator's coordinate-sum representation:\n.\n\nExpand symbolic advection as a finite coordinate sum and apply\n to each derivative term. The\nanalytic operator is ; expanding  in the Euclidean\nbasis identifies it with that sum.\n\nFor momentum component , assume ValidAt at  for  and .\nThe Navier–Stokes bridge also assumes validity of every first symbolic partial\nof ; the conservation bridge assumes validity of every flux component.\nThese give the stationary Euler and Navier–Stokes momentum residuals and the\ndivergence-form conservation residual. Incompressibility is a separate equation.\nThe undifferentiated viscosity and source terms need no validity hypotheses\nfor these identities. Componentwise, the Euler bridge gives\n,\nwhile the Navier–Stokes bridge adds  and the\nconservation bridge gives\n.\n\nNormalize each reflected residual, rewrite its differentiated components using\n, and identify the transport term\nwith . The remaining sums and products\nevaluate definitionally.\n\nThe viscosity term here is specifically . For a\nvariable coefficient this differs from divergence-form diffusion:\n\n\n\nThe extra term vanishes for constant viscosity. Keeping the exact operator\nvisible matters when applying a residual bridge: a certificate for one of\nthese expressions says nothing about the other.\n\nIn positive dimension, lower a differential equation against a closed-form\nansatz to a symbolic residual. If that residual evaluates to zero at the point\nand its analytic side conditions hold there, then\n.\n\nRewrite the analytic residual as the evaluation of the lowered residual,\nusing the analytic side conditions. ClosedResidualVanishesAt is exactly the\nassertion that this evaluation is zero, so it finishes the proof. A side-aware\nnormalizer is one way to supply that vanishing proof after its denominator\nand branch conditions have been discharged.\n\nThe integration entry point accepts a candidate  only after checking\nthat  normalizes to zero. Its certificate records\nrequirements for the primitive, the integrand, and the conditional\ncancellations used in that check.Fix a coordinate line . At each point where those\nrequirements hold, the evaluated primitive has derivative\n. If they hold for every\n, thenThe interval hypothesis is Set.uIcc a b in Lean. It includes both\nendpoints and every point between them, regardless of their order.\nValidity of the integrand supplies continuity and hence interval\nintegrability; validity of the primitive supplies the derivative needed by\nthe fundamental theorem of calculus. Every search method uses this same\ncertificate. At the reflected API, IntegrationCheck.certifyCandidate?\ntakes the coordinate, original integrand, and proposed primitive explicitly,\nso it can check a candidate without running primitive search. The ordinary\ninterval tactic uses this boundary for a supplied function or a common\nfunction reconstructed from the requested endpoint difference. It reflects\nthe integrand and candidate over one parameter table, so each coefficient\nhas the same meaning in both expressions. Checking a candidate against its\nown derivative would not establish the requested integral.\n\nFor substitutions, the proportionality checker validates its proposed factor with\n. Polynomial, rational,\nsubstitution, and integration-by-parts passes only propose candidates. The\nintegration checker validates the symbolic derivative identity with\n. Under the certificate's\nrequirements,  turns it into\nan analytic derivative along . The interval-wide requirements also\nmake the evaluated integrand continuous there, so the fundamental theorem\ngives the endpoint difference.\n\nThe differentiation rule tree in detail.\n\nDiffRule names the differentiation rules, and DiffDerivation records\ntheir application to an expression. For an expression e and variable index\ni, this tree connects the raw derivative e.diff i to the analytic partial\nderivative. Its rules are:\n\n* Constant rule. Every rational literal, , and  differentiates to zero: .* Variable rule. A coordinate  differentiates to the Kronecker delta: .* Sum, difference, and negation. These distribute linearly: .* Product rule. For : the derivative is .* Quotient rule. For  with  at the evaluation point: the derivative is .* Natural power rule. For : the derivative is .* Real power rule. For  with  at the evaluation point: the derivative is , the two terms of Mathlib's HasDerivAt.rpow; a literal natural exponent is lowered to the natural power rule instead.* Transcendental chain rules. Each elementary function composes with the inner derivative :* ,* ,* ,*  and  (requiring ),* ,*  (requiring  under Mathlib's total real logarithm),*  (requiring ).\n\nThe raw derivative is simplified by evaluating constant arithmetic,\neliminating multiplication by zero or one, and removing addition of zero.\nThese transformations preserve its value.\nA theorem may request a form that this simplifier does not reach, such as a\nfactored heat-kernel derivative or a square-root expression needing\n. The derivative tactic then tries the side-conditioned\nnormalizer, whose real-power and square-root rules identify the two forms\nunder the recorded conditions.\n\nExample: differentiating .\n\nConsider the expression  with variables  and\n in . Differentiating with respect to :\n\n1. The outermost constructor is sin, so the sine chain rule applies.2. The inner expression is , which uses the product rule.3. Differentiating  gives 1 (variable rule, Kronecker delta).4. Differentiating  with respect to  gives 0 (variable rule).5. The product rule yields .6. The chain rule produces .7. The simplifier reduces this to .\n\nThe bridge theorem then proves  at every point , as an identity in Lean's real analysis library.\n\nSupported antiderivative patterns.\n\nThe bounded antiderivative search IntegrationPlanner.candidateWithFuel?\ncombines seven mechanisms.\n\nPolynomial integration. Sparse multivariate polynomials are integrated\nterm by term. Each monomial  integrates in coordinate  to . The sparse representation avoids allocating zero coefficients.\n\nCalibrated substitution. For an integrand , the planner collects a list of substitution primitives. These include  for quotients by , inverse-power primitives, , , and . For each primitive, the proportionality checker (Alg.proportionality?) verifies whether the integrand equals the primitive's derivative times a factor independent of the integration coordinate. If successful, the candidate is that constant factor times the primitive.\n\nIntegration by parts. The LIATE heuristic ranks factors by their\nsuitability for differentiation. Logarithmic and inverse-trigonometric factors\nreceive rank 5, algebraic polynomial factors rank 4, trigonometric factors rank 3, and exponential factors rank 2.\nFor a product , the planner recursively integrates , forms , recursively integrates the correction, and validates the result. The initial recursion fuel is 96; this bounds recursive depth, not the total number of calls across search branches.\n\nExponential-trigonometric recurrences. For integrands of the form , the planner solves a finite coupled polynomial recurrence. Writing the antiderivative as , the amplitudes satisfy\n\n\n\nWhen , the constant matrix is invertible, and repeated matrix inversion lowers the polynomial degree. The process terminates in at most  steps.\n\nThe implemented route allows multivariate rational-polynomial amplitudes,\nwith the other coordinates treated as parameters, and common exponential and\ntrigonometric phases whose slopes in the selected coordinate are rational\nconstants.\n\nTrigonometric powers. The planner proposes primitives for sine and cosine\npowers with every fixed natural exponent. For positive powers, the phase\nderivative must simplify to an expression independent of the selected\ncoordinate and different from literal zero. The final certificate records the\nnonvanishing conditions needed for division. For constant nonzero slope\n, the low-power cases include:\n\n* ,* ,* ,* .\n\nRational function integration. For univariate rational functions , the pipeline proceeds in three stages:\n\n* Polynomial division. Exact sparse Euclidean division separates the improper part:  with . The polynomial quotient  is integrated term by term.* Hermite reduction. The proper fraction  undergoes Yun square-free decomposition of the denominator. For each repeated factor , the Hermite recurrence lowers the multiplicity by one at each step, producing rational correction terms that do not require logarithms.* Elementary transcendental integration. The terminal square-free fraction\n    uses partial-fraction decomposition over the rationals. Linear factors give\n    logarithms. For a quadratic denominator , separate off the\n    logarithmic-derivative part. The remaining primitive uses arctangent when\n     and real logarithms when .\n\nThe rational factor search combines rational-root extraction and exact\nKronecker search over Rat; extended Euclid supplies the modular inverses.\nIrrational scales in quadratic primitives remain exact symbolic square roots.\n\nBronstein (2005, Chapter 2) gives the rational-integration methods behind these\nstages. Here they are used in a bounded candidate search, and the accepted\nprimitive's derivative is checked.\n\nCompositional linearity. Sums, differences, negations, and scalar-independent multiples are handled compositionally.\n\nBefore a certificate's nonzero conditions reach a proof, a checked compactor\ncollects square-root products, extracts common factors, splits products and\npowers, normalizes factors, and deduplicates them. Its soundness theorem\nrecovers every raw obligation from the compact list.\n\nExample: integrating a rational function.\n\nConsider . The algebra to be checked is\n\n\n\nThus, on any interval avoiding  and , a candidate primitive is\n\n\n\nDifferentiating the middle term recovers the repeated pole; differentiating\nthe two logarithms recovers the simple poles. This concrete decomposition\nexplains the roles of the pipeline's stages:\n\n1. Polynomial division. The numerator has lower degree, so the quotient is zero.2. Square-free decomposition. The denominator factors as . Yun decomposition identifies  with multiplicity 2 and  with multiplicity 1.3. Hermite reduction. The recurrence absorbs the repeated factor, producing a rational correction and lowering the denominator to square-free form .4. Partial fractions. Modular inverse computation splits the residual into terms with denominators  and .5. Elementary integration. Each partial fraction  integrates to .6. Validation. The complete candidate is differentiated symbolically, and the residual is normalized to zero by the checked route. The requirement set records  and .\n\nTo use the endpoint formula, the entire closed interval must avoid both\npoles. Checking only  and  is insufficient: the\nendpoints ,  pass those tests but enclose the pole at .\nThis is why the integral theorem asks for nonvanishing at every point of\nSet.uIcc a b, while a pointwise derivative theorem asks only at its\nevaluation point.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Exact Symbolic Calculus","header":"10.6.2. Symbolic Calculus and Analytic Bridges","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Symbolic-Calculus-and-Analytic-Bridges"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Checked-Antiderivatives":{"contents":"The integration command searches for a primitive and then verifies it:\n\n-- Search for a primitive, then verify it\n-- by differentiation.\n#cas [integrate] (sin x)\n\n\nIts compact report is:\n\n#cas [integrate]\nDomain: x ∈ ℝ\nInput (scalar ℝ): (sin x)\nOutput (scalar ℝ; antiderivative): -cos(x)\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe detailed calculation has four mathematical stages:\n\n1. differentiate the proposed primitive using the indexed symbolic rules;2. subtract the original integrand to form a residual;3. prove that the residual normalizes to zero;4. retain every nonzero or positivity requirement needed by the analytic\n   derivative theorem.\n\nFor this example there are no side conditions. An accepted antiderivative\ncertificate supplies both a pointwise derivative identity and its\ncoordinate-line fundamental theorem of calculus consequence. The ordinary\ntactic chooses how to prove the stated goal. The elementary HasDerivAt\nbranch checks the function already in the goal. An interval-integral\nequality can provide a primitive through its endpoint expression, or leave\nthe bounded planner to find one. These are separate tactic calls:\n\n-- Check the stated function's derivative.\nexample (x : ℝ) :\n    HasDerivAt\n      (fun t : ℝ ↦ -Real.cos t)\n      (Real.sin x) x := by\n  cas\n\n-- Search for a primitive and apply the fundamental theorem of calculus.\nexample (a b : ℝ) :\n    (∫ t in a..b, Real.sin t) =\n      Real.cos a - Real.cos b := by\n  cas\n\n\nSubstitution can reuse the integration methods rather than guess another formula. For\n, choose . Dividing by  leaves , whose integral is\n. Substituting back gives ; differentiation certifies the result wherever\n. The same step works for a rational function of an exponential:\n\n#cas [integrate] (fun x ↦ log x / x)\n#cas [integrate] (fun x ↦ exp x / (1 + (exp x) ^ 2))\n\n\nFor the second expression,  gives , so the primitive is\n. Other variables remain coefficients. The rational calculation\n has primitive , without assigning values to\n and . Mixed sine–cosine powers first use . Rational expressions\nin a common affine trigonometric phase can use the tangent half-angle substitution; its\nprimitive may require a restricted interval to avoid a branch singularity.\n\nThe search runs in compiled code, and the kernel checks the proposed primitive against the\noriginal integrand. A division introduced during discovery does not, by itself, become a\nhypothesis: for , the primitive  is valid at  as well.\n\nSuppose the requested endpoint difference is F b - F a. When its two terms\nexhibit a common function, the tactic can try that function as a primitive.\nIt differentiates the candidate and checks the residual against the original\nintegrand. An unsuccessful automatic candidate leaves bounded search\navailable. A function supplied with cas [integrate primitive := F] is a\ndifferent contract: that candidate must pass, including the interval\nrequirements and the final endpoint equality.\n\nFor example, Real.log t ^ 2 / 2 is a useful proposed primitive for\nReal.log t / t. Its use requires that every point of Set.uIcc a b avoid\nzero. The interval includes both endpoints and works in either orientation.\nAdding a constant to the primitive leaves its endpoint difference unchanged;\nadding a constant to the requested integral value changes the proposition\nthat must be proved. Candidate selection cannot excuse that mismatch.\n\nThe integration engine is deliberately incomplete. It searches a bounded\ncollection of polynomial, rational, substitution, integration-by-parts,\nexponential, trigonometric, logarithmic, and quadratic-terminal patterns.\nFinding a candidate is not enough: failure of the residual check or any\nrequired side condition makes the tactic fail without changing the goal.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.4. Checked Antiderivatives","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Checked-Antiderivatives"}});